# 6. Capability

A Capability groups multiple Features that together provide a complete business capability.

Examples:

- Customer Registration
- Receipt Processing
- Reward Redemption
- Campaign Management
- Business Reporting

Capabilities are planning artifacts.

They are never assigned directly to implementation agents.

---

# 7. Feature

A Feature represents one business capability that can be demonstrated.

Examples:

- OTP Login
- Join Loyalty Program
- Record Receipt
- Redeem Reward Points
- Surprise Reward
- Birthday Benefit

Every Feature belongs to exactly one Capability.

---

# 8. Task

Tasks are the smallest independently assignable implementation units.

Every Task must:

- have exactly one owner;
- belong to one module;
- produce measurable output;
- have clear acceptance criteria;
- define mandatory tests;
- define review requirements.

Tasks should normally require between four and sixteen engineering hours.

Large Features should always be decomposed into multiple Tasks.

---

# 9. Subtask

Subtasks exist only to simplify implementation.

Examples:

- create migration
- implement repository
- implement service
- create DTO
- add API endpoint
- write unit tests
- write integration tests
- update documentation

Subtasks are optional.

---

# 10. Task Identifier

Every implementation Task receives a permanent identifier.

Format:

```
LP-0001
LP-0002
LP-0003
```

Identifiers are never reused.

Cancelled tasks remain part of project history.

---

# 11. Standard Task Structure

Every implementation task must contain the following information.

## Identification

- Task ID
- Title
- Epic
- Capability
- Feature

## Business Context

- Business Objective
- Business Value
- Business Rules

## Technical Context

- Owning Module
- Dependencies
- Required Blueprint Documents
- Required Engineering Documents
- Knowledge Package

## Implementation Scope

- Exact Scope
- Out of Scope
- Allowed Files
- Forbidden Files

## Validation

- Acceptance Criteria
- Definition of Done
- Mandatory Tests
- UAT References

## Reviews

- Required Review Agents
- Security Review
- Documentation Review

## Delivery

- Expected Deliverables
- Rollback Expectations

---

# 12. Task Ownership

Every Task has exactly one owning implementation role.

Examples:

- Backend Developer Agent
- Database Agent
- Customer App Agent
- Employee App Agent
- QA Agent
- Security Agent
- DevOps Agent
- Documentation Agent

Ownership must never be shared.

Multiple agents may collaborate, but responsibility remains singular.

---

# 13. Task Dependencies

Dependencies must be explicit.

Supported dependency types:

- blocks
- blocked by
- requires
- follows
- parallel with

Hidden dependencies are not permitted.

---

# 14. Task States

Tasks progress through the following lifecycle.

```
Draft

↓

Ready

↓

Assigned

↓

In Progress

↓

Implementation Complete

↓

Review

↓

QA

↓

Ready for Merge

↓

Merged

↓

Done
```

Alternative states:

- Blocked
- Cancelled

---

# 15. Task Priority

Priority indicates implementation urgency.

| Priority | Meaning |
|-----------|---------|
| P0 | Critical |
| P1 | High |
| P2 | Normal |
| P3 | Low |

Priority is independent from implementation complexity.

---

# 16. Complexity Scale

Implementation complexity is estimated using:

| Level | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| XS | Very Small |
| S | Small |
| M | Medium |
| L | Large |
| XL | Very Large |
| XXL | Requires decomposition |

Tasks estimated as XXL must be split before implementation.

---

# 17. AI Context Size

Every task defines its expected context size.

Possible values:

- Small
- Medium
- Large
- Very Large

This helps AI agents optimize context loading.

---

# 18. Parallel Development Rules

Tasks may execute in parallel only when:

- they belong to different modules;
- they do not modify the same aggregate;
- they do not change shared contracts;
- dependency graph permits parallel work.

Parallel work must never introduce ownership conflicts.

---

# 19. Knowledge Package Requirement

Every implementation task must reference exactly one primary Knowledge Package defined in:

**58-project-knowledge-map.md**

The Knowledge Package determines which documents the implementation agent must load before beginning work.

---

# 20. Traceability

Every Task must maintain complete traceability.

Minimum references:

- Blueprint documents
- Engineering documents
- Module
- Events
- APIs
- Database objects
- UAT scenarios
- Definition of Done
- Pull Request
- Release

Traceability must remain valid throughout the project lifecycle.

---

# 21. Backlog Maintenance

The Product Owner owns backlog prioritization.

The Solution Architect owns technical decomposition.

Implementation Agents may suggest:

- decomposition;
- dependency corrections;
- estimation changes;
- documentation updates.

Implementation Agents may not reprioritize backlog items.

---

# 22. AI Backlog Rules

AI Agents receive work only through approved Tasks.

Agents must never:

- invent Tasks;
- merge multiple Tasks without approval;
- silently expand scope;
- skip required reviews;
- modify backlog priorities.

If additional work is discovered, the agent must propose a new Task instead of expanding the current one.

---

# 23. Definition of Backlog Ready

A backlog item is Ready when:

- Product Decision exists;
- Blueprint references exist;
- Engineering references exist;
- owning module is defined;
- dependencies are identified;
- Knowledge Package is assigned;
- acceptance criteria are complete;
- Definition of Done is defined;
- mandatory tests are identified;
- required reviewers are assigned;
- rollback expectations are documented.

Only Ready Tasks may be assigned to implementation agents.

---

# 24. Definition of Product Backlog Ready

The Product Backlog is considered ready when:

- all implementation Epics are defined;
- all Capabilities are mapped;
- all Features belong to an Epic;
- Task structure is standardized;
- ownership rules are defined;
- dependency rules are defined;
- AI assignment rules are defined;
- traceability rules are defined;
- backlog governance is defined;
- backlog supports Blueprint v1.0 and Engineering Playbook v1.0.

The Product Backlog becomes the authoritative source for implementation planning after Blueprint Freeze v1.0.